Skip System.gc() while no weak reference holds an object and memory is free.

Games call System.gc() several times a second, many before every image they
load, and each call ran a full collection, which took several percent of the
processor time. A collection changes what a program can observe only through
the weak references it clears and the memory it frees. So the call returns at
once while a quarter of the heap is free and no weak reference holds an
object, apart from the JAR file parsers that the VM caches weakly and
reopens when it loses one. A weak reference to an unreachable object, young
or old, is cleared as before; with less free memory the whole heap is
collected, because some games compare freeMemory() after System.gc() with a
few kilobytes. Finalizers of dead weak references wait for the next
allocation-triggered collection. Builds with soft references collect as
before.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/share/memory/ObjectHeap.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/share/memory/ObjectHeap.cpp
@@ -902,6 +902,22 @@
 #endif
 }
 
+static bool found_weak_referent;
+
+static void note_weak_referent(OopDesc** p) {
+  // The VM caches parsers of JAR files weakly; losing one only means that
+  // the file is opened again.
+  if (!(*p)->is_jar_file_parser()) {
+    found_weak_referent = true;
+  }
+}
+
+bool ObjectHeap::has_weak_referents() {
+  found_weak_referent = false;
+  weak_refs_do(note_weak_referent);
+  return found_weak_referent;
+}
+
 #if USE_SOFT_REFERENCES
 inline void ObjectHeap::soft_refs_do(void do_oop(OopDesc**)) {
